<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  One entitlement, and the absence of the other one is the security property.

	  **There is deliberately no keychain-access-groups here.** The same rule the watch
	  complication follows: the extension cannot read or write the group the accounts live in,
	  and that is enforced by the system rather than promised in a comment. Anyone auditing this
	  should check that this file still has exactly one key.

	  This does not mean the extension cannot use the Keychain at all. It still has the default
	  group derived from its bundle identifier, and on iOS the app group above doubles as a
	  Keychain access group. The app writes to neither, so nothing is exposed. See VERIFYING.md,
	  which states this accurately, and audit X2, OF-A7.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.dev.openfactor</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
